fix(hwmon): drive Apple Silicon fans through macsmc_hwmon

The Apple Silicon path written for macsmc-hwmon never runs on the driver
that landed in Linux 6.19. The kernel registers the hwmon device as
macsmc_hwmon, with an underscore, so the name check never matches and the
fans fall through to the generic path as rpm-only, "not controllable:
NoPwm". Even with the name matched, detection demanded a writable
fanN_manual, which this driver does not have. The first in-range write to
fanN_target switches a fan to manual, and writing 0 hands it back to the
SMC firmware. Any other value is refused with EINVAL.

On macsmc_hwmon alone, the name is the kernel's, detection needs no
manual file, a duty maps onto the fan's own minimum to maximum, 0% writes
0 so the firmware owns the fan again (only the firmware can stop it), the
switch to manual writes nothing, and the reset writes 0.

Shutdown reset every fan through pwm_enable, which a fan without one skips
as a success, so an Apple Silicon fan kept the last duty the daemon set
after it stopped. A controllable macsmc_hwmon fan is now reset through the
Apple path. The Intel applesmc path is left exactly as it was, including
at shutdown, where it has the same gap. That is a separate change.

Measured on a J316c MacBook Pro (M1 Max), kernel 7.1.13, fan_control=1,
with fan1 at 1499 to 5348 rpm, fan2 at 1499 to 5776 rpm, the hwmon name
macsmc_hwmon and no fanN_manual.

